#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073434 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line"}] :question/id #uuid "2ce03c34-8e2c-5554-a129-1eaf05ad46fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12548" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(3,2)$ and the line $x=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](images/95/952be9b27e5350f755f0f534883f3c70516bb4e03b34e9448ab5d43950d4bad6.png)\n\nSince the line $x = 5$ is vertical, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(3,2)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $x$-coordinate of the point and $5.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd = |3 - 5| = 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073435 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "64a6782b-aa64-5027-8311-3498c19c39e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12549" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(1,3)$ and the line $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](images/5c/5ccfa3c27c6bb2759ffc37c73b3640583fa962ee79f47b21f02ac0c1d9164b00.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x - 5$ and passes through the point $(1,3).$ \n\nThe given line has a slope of $\\dfrac{1}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-2.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-2(x-1) \\\\ y-3 & =-2x+2 \\\\ y & =-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{1}{2}x-5 \\\\ y &=-2x+5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by setting the two equations equal and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-5 & =-2x+5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+2x & =5+5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}x & =10 \\\\ x & =10\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-3.$ So, our solution is $x=4$ and $y=-3.$ Consequently, the lines intersect at the point $\\left( 4,-3\\right).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(1,3)$ and $\\left( 4, -3 \\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(4-1)}^{2}+{(-3-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9}\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(1,3)$ and the line $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$ is $3 \\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "57fc631b-c251-55d2-a9d5-6b41fc4bb00c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12612" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c48fc163b3c9543dcfdd03b20882f0d2a19747672c6b1db2a42a683f0014fe25.png)\n\n \n\nFind the point on the line $y-x=4$ that is closest to the point $(3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-x & =4 \\\\ y & =x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y =  x + 4$ and passes through the point $(3,3).$ \n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-1(x-3) \\\\ y-3 & =-x+3 \\\\ y & =-x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=x+4 \\\\ y &=-x+6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =-x+6 \\\\ x+x & =6-4 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=5.$ So, our solution is $x=1$ and $y=5.$ Consequently, the lines intersect at the point $(1,5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y-x= 4$ that is closest to the point $(3,3)$ is  $(1,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0498568d-b327-5f78-89e8-8587c0368b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339162} {:answer/id #uuid "850104ea-f24f-5eb3-bc01-1f1701a3ca6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340323} {:answer/id #uuid "a884e107-b09a-542c-94ff-f2323802311a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340660} {:answer/id #uuid "c8516292-870e-5d6a-9fc2-73422fa2795d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340902} {:answer/id #uuid "d93f1851-2a54-53fb-9fee-5fb53f3f5e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a884e107-b09a-542c-94ff-f2323802311a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340660} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1068806-46ea-595d-a611-e82e67e43211" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2a5b92a-6d2c-561b-999b-9dedaf72311f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74060" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f97972b1765e58a76475a99a97e4a8818b05efa1f26a79a232b250b6ef681e9b.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $2y-3x=3$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3x & =3 \\\\ 2y & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}$ and passes through the point $(2,-2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-2) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y &=-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{6}{13}\\cdot \\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=0.$ So, our solution is $x=-1$ and $y=0.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $2y-3x=3$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2)$ is  $(-1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6304ff82-aa6c-50b7-b79e-a11e972b7aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340009} {:answer/id #uuid "7d839962-7657-5bb7-904b-97d127c09ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{9}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340259} {:answer/id #uuid "a38a60c9-c99e-5333-a142-a09672caa6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340611} {:answer/id #uuid "befaa2f8-684a-5c95-bb90-4e92f8bb8e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340830} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc6bc70-eb00-5071-8312-8709fa64cf86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6304ff82-aa6c-50b7-b79e-a11e972b7aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340009} :answer-field/id #uuid "f952c9dc-1256-5a8a-9688-3f9bfd070213" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "853cfa3c-da08-562e-b7e9-24b69cddbfcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-74061" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d478fb4499ee19a7c385ae93dd5ad790404351ceda63c20341b9996a1466ca60.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ that is closest to the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ and passes through the point $(2,1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{3}{2}x-2 \\\\ y &=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}x \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-2.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=-2.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(0,-2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ that is closest to the point $(2,1)$ is $(0,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59078a96-a4c1-5355-9bdf-3b0f39c93291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339938} {:answer/id #uuid "836328cc-8806-5c45-bf00-1c3923682d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340310} {:answer/id #uuid "9909d2b8-e7fa-56b5-9269-e5951dc53bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340509} {:answer/id #uuid "a86ba75a-1b29-5797-9053-bf2a3ad7aa27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340658} {:answer/id #uuid "b0de169a-6a53-5b29-85e4-09cc91632445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59078a96-a4c1-5355-9bdf-3b0f39c93291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339938} :answer-field/id #uuid "cab68815-1850-5693-b986-be3ac48add05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79f2f5ac-44c5-5b82-9c80-2524e0bd699c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74202" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-4)$ and the line $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](images/d4/d46a6ed76af5c226e327ba197fe019df0d9085e095ca4781898aba54268a1706.png)\n\nSince the line $x=4$ is a vertical line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(-1,-4)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $x$-coordinate of the point and $4.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|-1-4|=5.\n$$"}]}
